
difference between
| Feature                           | `Future` | `CompletableFuture`                   |
| --------------------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Manual completion                 | ❌        | ✅ (`complete(value)`)                 |
| Chaining (thenApply, thenCompose) | ❌        | ✅                                     |
| Combining multiple futures        | ❌        | ✅ (`thenCombine`, `allOf`, `anyOf`)   |
| Exception handling                | ❌        | ✅ (`exceptionally`, `handle`)         |
| Non-blocking callbacks            | ❌        | ✅ (`thenAcceptAsync`, `thenRunAsync`) |
| Custom Executor                   | ✅        | ✅                                     |
| Reactive style support            | ❌        | ✅                                     |

arraylist vs linkedlist
| Operation / Position       | ArrayList         | LinkedList | Explanation                                                                |
| -------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Insertion at Beginning** | O(n)              | O(1)       | ArrayList must shift all elements; LinkedList just updates a few pointers. |
| **Insertion in Middle**    | O(n)              | O(n)       | ArrayList shifts elements; LinkedList must traverse to that index.         |
| **Insertion at End**       | O(1)* (Amortized) | O(1)       | ArrayList sometimes resizes; LinkedList adds directly at tail.             |
| **Deletion at Beginning**  | O(n)              | O(1)       | ArrayList shifts all elements left; LinkedList updates head pointer.       |
| **Deletion in Middle**     | O(n)              | O(n)       | Both must traverse to index; ArrayList shifts elements afterward.          |
| **Deletion at End**        | O(1)              | O(1)       | ArrayList removes last element directly; LinkedList updates tail pointer.  |
| **Fetching (get(index))**  | O(1)              | O(n)       | ArrayList provides random access; LinkedList must traverse sequentially.   |
| **Search (contains)**      | O(n)              | O(n)       | Both require full traversal in worst case.                                 |

🔹 ArrayList

Backed by a dynamic array.

Indexed random access → fast get(index).

But insertion/deletion cause shifting of elements.

Resizing happens occasionally (doubling capacity), which makes insertion amortized O(1) at the end.

🔹 LinkedList

Backed by doubly linked nodes.

No random access → must traverse from start or end.

Insertions/deletions only need pointer changes → O(1) when the position is known.

Traversal makes middle operations slower.

| Use Case                                            | Recommended      |
| --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |
| Frequent insert/delete at **beginning or middle**   | ✅ **LinkedList** |
| Frequent **random access** or operations at **end** | ✅ **ArrayList**  |


map implementation

For each addition of a new entry to the map, check the load factor.
If it's greater than its pre-defined value (or default value of 0.75 if not given),
then Rehash.
For Rehash, make a new array of double the previous size and make it the new
bucketarray.
Then traverse to each element in the old bucketArray and call the insert()
for each so as to insert it into the new larger bucket array.



putIfAbsent(K key, V value)
Purpose:

Inserts the given key–value pair only if the key is not already present in the map.

If the key is already present, it does not overwrite the existing value.

Return Value:

Returns the previous value associated with the key,

Or null if there was no mapping for the key before.

🔹 2. getOrDefault(K key, V defaultValue)

Purpose:

Retrieves the value for a given key.

If the key is not found, returns the default value provided.

Does not modify the map.

Return Value:

The value associated with the key if present,

Otherwise the provided default value.